From c2e28d808c9b992c1ca4152449795a7734b12a0b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shardul Bankar <shardul.b@mpiricsoftware.com>
Date: Fri, 22 May 2026 22:13:09 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] mptcp: initialize BRF kcov fields in __mptcp_init_sock

The BRF kcov fields on struct mptcp_sock (kcov_remote_handle,
kcov_coverage_area, kcov_area_size, kcov_owner) are written only
by setsockopt(MPTCP_KCOV_HANDLE); __mptcp_init_sock() never
initialized them.

mptcp_prot carries SLAB_TYPESAFE_BY_RCU, so sk_prot_alloc()
strips __GFP_ZERO and a recycled mptcp_sock is not re-zeroed.  An
msk that never sets a kcov handle -- a plain fuzzer-created MPTCP
socket, or a listener the harness deliberately leaves unset --
therefore carries stale bytes from the slab object's prior
occupant.  BRF_MPTCP_KCOV_START sees a non-zero kcov_remote_handle
and non-NULL kcov_coverage_area, feeds the garbage handle into
kcov_remote_start_prealloc(), and kcov_check_handle() trips
WARN_ON at kernel/kcov.c:971.  Slab churn made this fire ~24x per
fuzz run.

Initialize the four fields in __mptcp_init_sock() under
CONFIG_KCOV.  That is the single init path for every msk -- both
mptcp_init_sock() (new sockets) and mptcp_sk_clone_init()
(accepted sockets, called after the sk_clone_lock() copy) reach
it -- so it also subsumes the accept-path clearing from the kcov
use-after-free fix (patch 0006), now reduced to a comment.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-4-7
Signed-off-by: Shardul Bankar <shardul.b@mpiricsoftware.com>
---
 net/mptcp/protocol.c | 35 ++++++++++++++++++++++++-----------
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)

diff --git a/net/mptcp/protocol.c b/net/mptcp/protocol.c
index bdd2ef3027d45..bf16b4489cd64 100644
--- a/net/mptcp/protocol.c
+++ b/net/mptcp/protocol.c
@@ -3053,6 +3053,24 @@ static void __mptcp_init_sock(struct sock *sk)
 	mptcp_pm_data_init(msk);
 	spin_lock_init(&msk->fallback_lock);
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_KCOV
+	/* mptcp_prot uses SLAB_TYPESAFE_BY_RCU, so sk_prot_alloc() strips
+	 * __GFP_ZERO and a recycled mptcp_sock carries stale bytes in any
+	 * field not explicitly initialised here.  The BRF kcov fields are
+	 * only written by setsockopt(MPTCP_KCOV_HANDLE); an msk that never
+	 * sets a handle (e.g. a plain fuzzer-created socket, or a listener
+	 * the harness deliberately leaves unset) would otherwise expose
+	 * garbage to BRF_MPTCP_KCOV_START, which feeds the handle straight
+	 * into kcov_remote_start_prealloc() -> kcov_check_handle() WARN.
+	 * Clearing them here is the single init path for every msk and
+	 * also covers the sk_clone_lock() accept path.
+	 */
+	msk->kcov_remote_handle = 0;
+	msk->kcov_coverage_area = NULL;
+	msk->kcov_area_size = 0;
+	msk->kcov_owner = NULL;
+#endif
+
 	/* re-use the csk retrans timer for MPTCP-level retrans */
 	timer_setup(&sk->mptcp_retransmit_timer, mptcp_retransmit_timer, 0);
 	timer_setup(&msk->sk.mptcp_tout_timer, mptcp_tout_timer, 0);
@@ -3572,18 +3590,13 @@ struct sock *mptcp_sk_clone_init(const struct sock *sk,
 
 	msk = mptcp_sk(nsk);
 
-#ifdef CONFIG_KCOV
-	/* sk_clone_lock() memcpy'd the listener's BRF kcov fields into
-	 * this accepted msk.  The kcov scratch area is owned by exactly
-	 * one msk -- do NOT inherit the pointer, or this msk would hold
-	 * a dangling reference once the owner frees it.  The harness
-	 * sets the handle explicitly per msk if it wants coverage here.
+	/* The BRF kcov fields that sk_clone_lock() memcpy'd from the
+	 * listener msk have already been cleared by __mptcp_init_sock()
+	 * above: the scratch area is owned by exactly one msk, so an
+	 * accepted msk must not inherit the listener's pointer.  The
+	 * harness sets the handle explicitly per msk if it wants
+	 * coverage on the accepted connection.
 	 */
-	msk->kcov_remote_handle = 0;
-	msk->kcov_coverage_area = NULL;
-	msk->kcov_area_size = 0;
-	msk->kcov_owner = NULL;
-#endif
 	WRITE_ONCE(msk->local_key, subflow_req->local_key);
 	WRITE_ONCE(msk->token, subflow_req->token);
 	msk->in_accept_queue = 1;
-- 
2.34.1

